{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_014KNNzcXcZNfeDEEPY2jSMA","type":"tool_result","content":"21:It was written by Lodestar Ledgers. It is not a leaked exam, not a reconstruction of one, and not a prediction of your score. Every question here is answerable from material this book taught, which is a property no real exam has.\n769:Authentication (who are you), then authorization (may you), then admission (should this specific object be allowed, and does it need modifying). *[cross-bearing: see Ch 8 §2 — three gates and a logbook]*\n915:`NoExecute` evicts running Pods that do not tolerate the taint, as well as preventing new ones. *[cross-bearing: see Ch 7 §4 — when the berth refuses you]*\n995:`maxSurge: 1` permits one Pod above the desired count, so at most five. `maxUnavailable: 0` means no old Pod is removed until a new one is ready; the surge Pod has to come first. *[cross-bearing: see Ch 6 §4 — changing the fleet under way]*\n1073:- **D is wrong** because a rolling update replaces instances incrementally without steering traffic by share.\n1117:Admission runs after authentication and authorization, and may reject or mutate the object before persistence. *[cross-bearing: see Ch 8 §2 — three gates and a logbook]*\n1240:Those two readers have the same score and should steer completely differently for the next two weeks. That is the entire argument for the table above, and it is why the Linux Foundation's own score report, which gives you a number and not a breakdown `[source: lf-exam-scoring-and-notification-2026-08-31]`, cannot do this job for you afterward."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f9e562b9-6f11-4061-8ae7-75124f562b56","uuid":"6f7fd8ec-ec40-48f1-ba89-e2165196a49d","timestamp":"2026-09-01T00:13:23.143Z","tool_use_result":{"mode":"content","numFiles":0,"filenames":[],"content":"21:It was written by Lodestar Ledgers. It is not a leaked exam, not a reconstruction of one, and not a prediction of your score. Every question here is answerable from material this book taught, which is a property no real exam has.\n769:Authentication (who are you), then authorization (may you), then admission (should this specific object be allowed, and does it need modifying). *[cross-bearing: see Ch 8 §2 — three gates and a logbook]*\n915:`NoExecute` evicts running Pods that do not tolerate the taint, as well as preventing new ones. *[cross-bearing: see Ch 7 §4 — when the berth refuses you]*\n995:`maxSurge: 1` permits one Pod above the desired count, so at most five. `maxUnavailable: 0` means no old Pod is removed until a new one is ready; the surge Pod has to come first. *[cross-bearing: see Ch 6 §4 — changing the fleet under way]*\n1073:- **D is wrong** because a rolling update replaces instances incrementally without steering traffic by share.\n1117:Admission runs after authentication and authorization, and may reject or mutate the object before persistence. *[cross-bearing: see Ch 8 §2 — three gates and a logbook]*\n1240:Those two readers have the same score and should steer completely differently for the next two weeks. That is the entire argument for the table above, and it is why the Linux Foundation's own score report, which gives you a number and not a breakdown `[source: lf-exam-scoring-and-notification-2026-08-31]`, cannot do this job for you afterward.","numLines":7,"totalLines":7}}
